**Goal** — Add a chunk-safe SGR mouse parser and a filtered Ink-compatible input stream with ordered mouse subscriptions and disposal.

**Motivation** — #2710 — the transcript cannot be scrolled with the mouse.

**Approach** — **3. Mouse input (US-002 module, US-003 wiring, #2710).** New module `src/tui/chat/mouse-input.ts`:

```ts
export const ENABLE_MOUSE = "\u001b[?1003h\u001b[?1006h";
export const DISABLE_MOUSE = "\u001b[?1006l\u001b[?1003l";

export type MouseButton = "left" | "middle" | "right" | "none";
export interface MouseEvent {
  readonly kind: "press" | "release" | "drag" | "move" | "wheelUp" | "wheelDown";
  readonly button: MouseButton;
  /** 1-based terminal column and row, as the terminal reports them. */
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
}
export interface MouseEvents {
  subscribe(listener: (event: MouseEvent) => void): () => void;
}
export interface MouseInput {
  /** What Ink reads: the source with every complete SGR mouse report removed. */
  readonly stdin: NodeJS.ReadStream;
  readonly events: MouseEvents;
  dispose(): void;
}
export function parseSgrMouse(sequence: string): MouseEvent | undefined;
export function createMouseInput(source: NodeJS.ReadStream): MouseInput;
```

An SGR report is `ESC [ < b ; x ; y M` (press or motion) or `... m` (release). The low two bits of `b` name the button (0 left, 1 middle, 2 right, 3 none); bit 32 marks motion (`drag` when a button is held, `move` when `b & 3 === 3`); 64 is `wheelUp` and 65 `wheelDown`; modifier bits 4, 8 and 16 are ignored. Horizontal wheel reports (`b` 66 and 67) parse to `undefined` but, being complete reports, are still removed from the stream and emit no event. `parseSgrMouse` returns `undefined` for anything that is not one complete report.

`createMouseInput` returns a `PassThrough` stream that forwards Ink's control calls to the source — `isTTY` (a property), `setRawMode`, `ref` and `unref` (added to the stream, since a `PassThrough` has no `ref`/`unref`) — and whose own `setEncoding("utf8")` call also sets the stream's own encoding, so Ink's `read()` returns strings. Source chunks are decoded with a `StringDecoder("utf8")`, so a multi-byte character split across chunks is not corrupted. `MouseInput` also has `dispose(): void`, which removes the source listener. For every chunk the source emits, writes the chunk minus its complete SGR reports and emits one `MouseEvent` per report, in order. A chunk that ends inside a report (its tail begins with `ESC [ <` and has no `M`/`m` terminator yet) keeps that tail and prepends it to the next chunk. A trailing bare `ESC` or `ESC [` is never held, so a pressed Escape key reaches Ink at once. A sequence that begins with `ESC [ <` but breaks the grammar before a terminator is passed through unchanged.

**Scope** — In: the new mouse-input module and its isolated unit tests. Out: renderChat/ChatApp/TranscriptViewport wiring and wheel reducers (US-003), selection/clipboard primitives and screen behavior (US-004/005). No configuration key to turn mouse reporting off is added.

**Interface** — The TypeScript declarations above are the module contract, with `MouseInput.dispose(): void` included as required by the design's lifecycle paragraph. Inject no real terminal into tests: create fake Node-compatible read streams. Add no imports of this module to production consumers until US-003.

**Goal** — Wire the mouse input into the terminal screen and route wheel reports to clamped transcript scrolling.

**Motivation** — The TUI enables no mouse reporting, so on the alternate screen the terminal turns the wheel into bare Up/Down, which the input consumes as prompt history.

**Approach** — `renderChat` builds the mouse input from `process.stdin` (injected through `_renderChatDeps.stdin`), passes `stdin: mouse.stdin` to `render`, passes `mouse.events` to `ChatApp` as a new optional prop `mouse`, writes `ENABLE_MOUSE` after `render` returns (Ink has put the terminal in raw mode by then, so no report is echoed) when the source is a TTY, and in its one-shot restore (unmount and the process `exit` handler alike) writes `DISABLE_MOUSE` right before `LEAVE_ALT_SCREEN` and calls `mouse.dispose()`.

**Wheel scroll.** `ChatApp` hands `mouse` to `TranscriptViewport` (new optional prop `mouse`). The viewport subscribes while it is active; `wheelUp` reduces the new viewport action `{ type: "wheelUp" }` and `wheelDown` `{ type: "wheelDown" }`, which move `WHEEL_LINES = 3` lines through the same `settle` clamp the line actions use (toward older lines for `wheelUp`). The wheel scrolls wherever the pointer is. Keyboard scrolling and the footer are unchanged.

**Scope** — In: renderChat filtered input and mouse enable/disable/dispose lifecycle, optional mouse props, viewport wheel actions and mounted seams. Out: implementing the parser/filter (US-002), selection helpers/clipboard (US-004), drag selection and copied hint (US-005), model choices/filter (US-006/007). No configuration key to turn mouse reporting off is added. The Ink version is not changed.

**Interface**
```ts
// Add to _renderChatDeps:
stdin: () => NodeJS.ReadStream;
createMouseInput: typeof createMouseInput;
// Add to ChatAppProps and TranscriptViewportProps:
readonly mouse?: MouseEvents;
// Add to ViewportAction:
{ type: "wheelUp" } | { type: "wheelDown" };
```
Target render expression: `render(createElement(ChatApp, { ...props, mouse: mouse.events }), { exitOnCtrlC: false, incrementalRendering: true, stdin: mouse.stdin })`. Write `ENTER_ALT_SCREEN` before render. For non-TTY stdin write neither ENABLE_MOUSE nor DISABLE_MOUSE; ordinary input continues through the filtered stream. Preserve existing renderer-throw restoration.

**Regression migration** — In test/ui/chat/index.test.tsx, stubScreen injects fake stdin and a fake MouseInput with recorded dispose; no test touches process.stdin. Render-options equality gains stdin equal to the fake mouse stream. TTY write order becomes [ENTER_ALT_SCREEN, ENABLE_MOUSE, DISABLE_MOUSE, LEAVE_ALT_SCREEN], with ENABLE_MOUSE after render; non-TTY stays [ENTER_ALT_SCREEN, LEAVE_ALT_SCREEN]. Restore remains one-shot.

**Goal** — Add pure display-cell selection primitives and a portable clipboard writer, with inverse span rendering.

**Motivation** — A tmux probe on 2026-10-27 confirmed Claude Code v2.1 enables any-motion tracking with SGR encoding on its alternate screen and draws and copies its own selection; the user ruled the TUI does the same (wheel scroll on, in-app drag selection).

**Approach** — **4. Selection and copy (US-004 modules, US-005 wiring, #2710).** A pure module `src/tui/chat/selection.ts`:

```ts
export interface TranscriptPoint { readonly line: number; readonly column: number }
export interface Selection { readonly anchor: TranscriptPoint; readonly focus: TranscriptPoint }
export interface ViewportGeometry {
  readonly top: number;      // 0-based screen row of the viewport's first row
  readonly height: number;   // viewport rows
  readonly total: number;    // transcript lines
  readonly offset: number;   // ViewportState.offset
  readonly unseen: number;   // ViewportState.unseen
}
export function transcriptPointAt(geometry: ViewportGeometry, x: number, y: number): TranscriptPoint | undefined;
export function selectedText(lines: readonly StyledLine[], selection: Selection): string;
export function highlightLine(line: StyledLine, index: number, selection: Selection): StyledLine;
```

`transcriptPointAt` maps a 1-based terminal cell to a transcript line and 0-based display column using the same window `ViewportRows` draws: `visibleRange(total, height, offset)`, the blank padding rows above short content, and the unseen-marker row (which is not a transcript line). Rows outside the viewport, padding rows and the marker row map to `undefined`. `selectedText` orders anchor and focus, takes each line's span text, slices the first and last line by display column (a wide grapheme partly inside the range is taken whole), trims trailing spaces per line and joins lines with `\n`. `highlightLine` returns the line with the selected columns split into spans carrying `inverse: true`; a line outside the selection is returned unchanged. `Span` gains `inverse?: boolean`, which `StyledLineView` passes to Ink's `Text` `inverse`.

Selection is inclusive of the focus cell.

New module `src/tui/chat/clipboard.ts`:

```ts
export const _clipboardDeps = {
  write: (text: string): void => { process.stdout.write(text); },
  platform: process.platform,
  spawnCopy: (text: string): Promise<number> => { /* Bun.spawn(["pbcopy"], { stdin }) exit code */ },
};
export function osc52(text: string): string; // "\u001b]52;c;" + base64(utf8 text) + "\u0007"
export async function copyToClipboard(text: string): Promise<void>;
```

`copyToClipboard` always writes `osc52(text)`; on `platform === "darwin"` it also runs `spawnCopy(text)`, because Terminal.app ignores OSC 52. A rejected or non-zero `spawnCopy` is swallowed (OSC 52 was already sent).

**Scope** — In: selection.ts, clipboard.ts, Span.inverse/StyledLineView, unit tests and existing styled-line rendering tests. Out: React gesture state, mouse subscription/wiring, top propagation and copied footer feedback (US-005); mouse parsing and scroll wiring (US-002/003). US-004 only: copying uses OSC 52 everywhere and `pbcopy` on macOS; no Linux or Windows clipboard command (`wl-copy`, `xclip`, `clip.exe`) is spawned.

**Interface** — Preserve the declarations above and add `readonly inverse?: boolean` to Span. Keep original styles while splitting selected runs. Tests isolate _clipboardDeps and use terminal display columns, including wide graphemes and reversed selections; no real clipboard subprocesses or stdout writes.

**Goal** — Connect mouse gestures to transcript selection/highlighting and clipboard copy with transient footer feedback.

**Motivation** — A tmux probe on 2026-10-27 confirmed Claude Code v2.1 enables any-motion tracking with SGR encoding on its alternate screen and draws and copies its own selection; the user ruled the TUI does the same (wheel scroll on, in-app drag selection).

**Approach** — `TranscriptViewport` owns the selection state: a `press` with `button: "left"` on a transcript point starts a selection (anchor = focus = that point) and clears any earlier one; a `drag` with `button: "left"` moves the focus to the point under the pointer (a drag outside the viewport keeps the last focus); a `release` copies when anchor and focus differ, by calling the new optional prop `onCopy(text)` with `selectedText`; the highlight stays until the next left press or until the transcript's line count changes (a re-render with the same number of lines, such as the working-line tick or a streamed token on the last line, keeps it). Selection is inclusive of the focus cell. `ChatApp` passes `top={HEADER_ROWS}` (3), the screen row of the viewport's first row. The press, drag and release state lives in a new hook `src/tui/chat/use-mouse-selection.ts` (`useMouseSelection`), which `TranscriptViewport` calls, so neither component grows a new branchy body. A press outside the transcript clears the selection and starts none. Drag-to-autoscroll past the viewport edge is out of scope.

`ChatApp` passes `onCopy` that calls `copyToClipboard` and shows the footer hint `copied <n> chars` (new `FooterHint` value `copied`, with the count) until the next key press or mouse press.

**Scope** — In: useMouseSelection, TranscriptViewport selection/highlight rendering, absolute top coordinate, ChatApp clipboard callback, copied footer hint and mounted/status regression tests. Out: parser/wheel plumbing (US-002/003), pure selection/clipboard implementation (US-004), catalog and picker filter changes (US-006/007). Guarded-mode permission posture (#2712) is the follow-up feature `tui-guarded-posture`; this feature does not change `chat-mode.ts`, `askRulesFor` or any permission or approval behaviour. US-005 only: drag-to-autoscroll when the pointer leaves the viewport during a selection is not implemented; the focus stays at the last point inside the viewport. US-005 only: double-click word selection, triple-click line selection and selecting text inside the input, ask card, picker, header or footer are not implemented.

**Interface**
```ts
// Add to TranscriptViewportProps:
readonly top?: number; // default 0; ChatApp passes HEADER_ROWS
readonly onCopy?: (text: string) => void;
// Add to FooterHint's existing union:
"copied";
// Add to footerModel input:
copiedChars?: number;
```
Consume `transcriptPointAt(geometry, x, y)`, `selectedText(lines, selection)` and `highlightLine(line, index, selection)` from US-004 and `MouseEvents` from US-002. Invoke `copyToClipboard(text): Promise<void>` from the ChatApp callback. A click (anchor equal to focus) makes no copy and shows no hint. Rejected/non-zero macOS spawnCopy is swallowed by clipboard.ts and does not prevent the copied hint. Add mounted seams driving fake MouseEvents at absolute screen coordinates, checking inverse rendering and stubbed _clipboardDeps.write OSC52 output; extend existing chat/viewport/status tests rather than touching the real terminal.

**Goal** — Expand native chat model choices from tier-only entries to ordered, deduplicated tiers, configured tool-capable overrides and tool-capable models from available providers.

**Motivation** — #2711 — `/model` offers three models.

**Approach** — **5. `/model` list (US-006, #2711).** New function in `src/agents/catalog/index.ts`:

```ts
export async function listCatalogModels(providerIds: readonly string[]): Promise<readonly string[]>;
```

It loads the bundled catalog through the existing cached `loadCatalog(_catalogDeps.loadProviders)` and returns `"<provider>/<id>"` for every model of each named provider whose `supportsTools` is true, providers in the order given, models in catalog order. A failed catalog load returns `[]`.

New module `src/chat/chat-models.ts`, exported from the chat barrel:

```ts
export interface ChatModelChoicesInput {
  readonly config: Pick<NaxConfig, "models" | "agent">;
  readonly availableProviders: readonly string[];
  readonly listCatalogModels: (providerIds: readonly string[]) => Promise<readonly string[]>;
}
export async function chatModelChoices(input: ChatModelChoicesInput): Promise<readonly string[]>;
```

Order: (a) the tier models `fast`, `balanced`, `powerful` exactly as `nativeModels` builds them today — `nativeModels` moves from `chat-host.ts` into `chat-models.ts` as the exported `tierModels(config)`, and `chat-host.ts` imports it; (b) every `agent.native.catalogOverrides[].models[]` entry whose `supportsTools` is true, as `"<provider>/<id>"`, in config order, whether or not its provider is in `availableProviders` (the user configured it on purpose); (c) `listCatalogModels(availableProviders)`. Duplicates keep their first position. A `listCatalogModels` rejection yields (a) then (b).

`ChatHostDeps` gains optional `models?: readonly string[]`; `NativeChatHost` uses it when present and the moved `tierModels(config)` otherwise. The old name `nativeModels(config)` in the baseline refers to this helper, not a second implementation to retain.

`composeChat` (`src/cli/chat.ts`) computes the list before `createChatHost`: `collectAuthList(requested)` (new `_chatCommandDeps.collectAuthList`), where `requested` is the provider of every tier model and every override (so a provider authenticated only through an environment variable is probed too), gives the providers with `available: true`, `chatModelChoices` (with new `_chatCommandDeps.listCatalogModels`) builds the list, and it is passed as `models`. A rejected `collectAuthList` is treated as no available provider.

**Scope** — In: catalog boundary listing, chat-models.ts and chat barrel export, optional host model injection, composeChat auth/list composition and fail-open behavior, CLI dependency-fixture updates, pure model/catalog tests, host fallback regressions and mounted picker navigation coverage. Out: model-picker typed filtering (US-007), all screen/mouse/rendering changes (US-001–005), changes to credential resolution or permission posture. US-006 only: ACP `modelChoices` (`packages/nax-agent-acp/src/server/session-config.ts`) keeps its tier-only list.

**Interface**
```ts
export function tierModels(config: Pick<NaxConfig, "models">): readonly string[];
// Add to ChatHostDeps:
readonly models?: readonly string[];
// Add to _chatCommandDeps:
collectAuthList: typeof collectAuthList;
listCatalogModels: typeof listCatalogModels;
```
The new tierModels signature retains the existing string/object formatting and first-position deduplication. `collectAuthList(providerIds): Promise<AuthListReport>` and `AuthListProvider.available: boolean` are existing read-only integration contracts. nax-ai `Catalog.listModels(provider?)` returns `ResolvedModel { id, provider, ... }`; supportsTools is the eligibility flag. Reuse ProviderCatalogOverrideSchema and CatalogModelOverrideSchema; add no config shape. Catalog/auth failures still leave tiers then eligible overrides. Stub both new deps in test/unit/cli/chat.test.ts and chat-resources.test.ts to empty provider reports and [] catalog results so existing tests never read credentials or load the real catalog, preserving existing assertions. Add pure helper/catalog tests and chatCommand seams asserting requested providers, available providers and models passed to the host. Preserve catalog caching and existing lifecycle cleanup.

**Goal** — Add model-only typed substring filtering whose state, row budget and rendered picker always agree.

**Motivation** — Follow-up ruling the same day: the `/model` list must be filterable by name, since it now holds dozens of rows.

**Approach** — **6. `/model` filter (US-007, #2711).** The filter lives in the picker's state, not in the `Picker` component, so the rows `ChatApp` reserves (`bottomRowsFor` → `pickerRows`) are the rows it draws. `PickerModelInput` and `PickerModel` gain `query?: string`. For `kind: "model"`, `pickerModel` keeps only the entries whose `value` contains the query case-insensitively (an empty or absent query keeps every entry) and always carries the query; the `mode` and `resume` kinds ignore it. A model picker draws one extra row under its title (counted by `fixedRows`, so `pickerRows` grows by one for `kind: "model"`): `Filter: <query>` when the query is non-empty, and the muted placeholder `type to filter` when it is empty; the row starts at column 0, never with the pointer or its blank. A model picker whose filter matches nothing draws the muted note `no models match`.

`ChatPicker` gains `setQuery(query: string): void`; `useChatPicker` stores the query with the open picker, resets it to `""` on `open` and on `cancel`/`choose`, and builds `model` from it. `PickerProps` gains optional `onQuery?: (query: string) => void` and `query?: string`; `ChatApp` passes `picker.setQuery` and `picker.model.query`. While a `kind: "model"` picker is active, `Picker` also owns: a printable input with no ctrl or meta modifier (appended to the query), and backspace or delete (the last grapheme removed; nothing happens on an empty query). Escape, return and the arrows keep their meaning. The pointer returns to the first entry whenever the query changes. Return on a picker with no entries does nothing.

**Scope** — In: Picker model/state/query interfaces, filter/note layout and row accounting, model-only typing/deletion and pointer reset, ChatApp prop wiring, and pure-layout/hook/mounted picker regression tests. Out: computing catalog/auth model choices (US-006), row slack/markdown (US-001), mouse/selection/clipboard (US-002–005). US-007 only: the `/resume` and `/mode` pickers do not filter; only the `/model` picker takes a typed query. US-007 only: the filter is a case-insensitive substring match on the model string; no fuzzy matching, ranking or highlighting of the matched characters.

**Interface**
```ts
// Add to PickerModelInput and PickerModel:
readonly query?: string;
// Add to ChatPicker:
setQuery(query: string): void;
// Add to PickerProps:
readonly query?: string;
readonly onQuery?: (query: string) => void;
```
Build the model from useChatPicker's query and pass `onQuery={picker.setQuery}` and `query={picker.model.query}` at ChatApp's Picker call site. Preserve existing key ownership and batched-key pointer behavior, including Escape's Ink meta flag and no choice on an empty filtered list. Extend test/ui/chat/picker.test.tsx with pure model/layout and mounted query seams: whole Switch model frame counts gain one row; entryRows, labels, (current) markers and choice assertions stay unchanged because the filter row starts at column 0.
